.TH KARIDNS\-DBLOADER 8 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns-dbloader.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns\-dbloader \- reads the PowerDNS 5.1 tables of a SQLite3 or PostgreSQL
database and hands the zones to the KariDNS Backend
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-dbloader \-\-drivers
karidns\-dbloader \-\-dump OUT \-\-driver sqlite3 \-\-database\-file PATH [options]
karidns\-dbloader \-\-dump OUT \-\-driver postgresql \-\-conninfo\-file FILE [\-\-schema NAME] [options]
karidns\-dbloader \-\-check \-\-driver sqlite3 \-\-database\-file PATH [options]
karidns\-dbloader \-\-check \-\-driver postgresql \-\-conninfo\-file FILE [\-\-schema NAME] [options]
karidns\-dbloader \-s FD [\-\-fetch\-fd FD] [\-\-state\-file PATH] [\-\-log\-stderr] [\-\-debug N]
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-s FD\fR is the mode \fBkaridns\fR runs; you do not start it by
hand. \fB\-\-drivers\fR, \fB\-\-dump\fR and \fB\-\-check\fR are for
administrators and for tests.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkaridns\fR never links a database library. When \fBoptions { database
{ ... }; }\fR (or a \fBview\fR database option) is configured, the manager
process starts \fB\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fB\fR (the loader) as a child. The loader
opens the database, reads the zones, converts every zone with the same readers
and checks as the zone-file parsers, and sends the result to the Backend over a
socket pair that the manager created before it dropped privileges (stream
\fBKDB1\fR, framed messages whose zone data is in Extended AXFR format). The
Backend builds the zones with the engine that also builds zone-file zones, so a
zone answers byte for byte the same from a file and from the database. See
\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "\fBdatabase { ... }\fR (in \fBoptions\fR) and the view
database options", for the configuration options and for everything a database
zone can carry (settings, ACLs, tags, secondary zones, ALIAS records).
.PP
What the loader does while \fBkaridns\fR runs:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStart.\fR It says HELLO (version, drivers), receives the configuration of the
databases from the Backend, asks for the Backend's inventory (the zones it
already has) and reads every zone. A zone whose content digest equals the
Backend's version is not sent. The first zones are served as soon as the first
batch part is published; a line \fBinitial load complete\fR is logged when the
first batch is done.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReload.\fR \fBkarictl reload\fR makes it compare the databases with the
inventory: new zones, zones whose SOA serial differs, zones the change log table
\fBkaridns_changes\fR lists as edited, and zones the Backend refused last time
are read and sent; zones that left the database get a delete. \fBkarictl reload
<zone>\fR reads that one zone. When a reload has to read more than
\fBbulk\-threshold\fR percent of the zones, one scan of all records replaces the
per-zone queries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAuto reload\fR (\fBauto\-reload yes;\fR). The loader polls (SQLite3) or
listens (PostgreSQL \fBLISTEN\fR) and sends a batch by itself after the first
commit plus the \fBcoalesce\fR window.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSeveral servers on one PostgreSQL database.\fR It reads
\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR and writes its outcome to
\fBkaridns_server_status\fR (see \fBkaridns(8)\fR, "Several servers on one
PostgreSQL database").
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSerial 0.\fR A zone whose SOA serial is 0 is loaded as it is; the loader logs
a warning (\fBthe SOA serial is 0; ...\fR) when it sends that version of the
zone, not again while the zone is unchanged. \fB\-\-check\fR reports the same as
a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCrash.\fR If the loader dies, \fBkaridns\fR keeps answering from the zones it
has and the manager starts a new loader (after 1 s, 2 s, 4 s ... 60 s; the delay
restarts after 10 minutes without a crash). A new loader compares \fBevery\fR
zone with the Backend's inventory before it reports its first batch (the whole
database is read once; see "Measurements").
.SS Privileges
.IP \(bu 2
The loader runs as \fBloader\-user\fR / \fBloader\-group\fR and \fBnever as
root\fR (\fBloader\-user\fR is required when \fBkaridns\fR starts as root). It
has no access to \fBkaridns.conf\fR, only to the database and to its socket;
everything else reaches it over the stream.
.IP \(bu 2
It is an ordinary process (not in a Capsicum sandbox): it needs the database
library and file or socket access, and what it reads from the database is
untrusted input that the shared reader validates. What it sends is untrusted for
the Backend, which validates the stream again and stays in capability mode: all
descriptors are opened by the manager at start, the Backend opens nothing new.
.IP \(bu 2
It reads only. The SQLite3 driver opens the file read-write (a reader of a WAL
database must write the \fB\-shm\fR file) but sets \fBPRAGMA query_only=1\fR, so
no statement of the connection can change data; PostgreSQL transactions are
\fBREAD ONLY\fR and the role should have no more than
\fBdb/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR gives it. The one thing the PostgreSQL
loader writes is its own row in \fBkaridns_server_status\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBloader\-path\fR (default \fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-dbloader\fR, or
\fBDIR/karidns\-dbloader\fR for a build with \fB./configure
\-\-libexecdir=DIR\fR) must be a regular executable file owned by root (or by
the user who runs \fBkaridns\fR when that is not root) and not writable by group
or others; \fBkaridns\fR checks this at start and before every
restart. \fBkaricheck conf\fR reports a problem in advance.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSQLite3:\fR the database must be in WAL mode (\fBPRAGMA journal_mode =
WAL\fR). A reader of a WAL database still writes the \fB\-shm\fR file, so
\fBloader\-user\fR needs read and write permission on the database file and on
its directory (to create \fB\-wal\fR and \fB\-shm\fR), and the file should not
be readable by everyone. \fBPostgreSQL:\fR the \fBconninfo\-file\fR has mode
0600 or 0400 and belongs to \fBloader\-user\fR; use a UNIX socket or
\fBsslmode=verify\-full\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkaricheck db\fR checks all of this in advance, from the same code (below).
.SS Drivers
The loader has the drivers it was built with: \fBkaridns\-dbloader
\-\-drivers\fR prints them, one per line, and the loader says them in its
HELLO. \fBkaridns\fR refuses \fBdatabase { driver x; }\fR when the installed
loader does not have \fBx\fR (and \fBkaricheck conf\fR says \fBdriver x is not
available in this installation\fR). To build:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
make karidns\-dbloader DB_DRIVERS="sqlite3 postgresql"
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBDB_DRIVERS\fR is any subset of \fBsqlite3 postgresql\fR (default
\fBsqlite3\fR); the SQLite3 driver needs \fBlibsqlite3\fR, the PostgreSQL driver
\fBlibpq\fR (PostgreSQL 13 or newer on the server). The FreeBSD packages are
described under "Packages".
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB\-\-dump OUT\fR
Write the stream exactly as the loader sends it to the Backend (HELLO,
BATCH_BEGIN, per zone ZONE_BEGIN / ZONE_DATA... / ZONE_END or ZONE_FAILED,
BATCH_END) to the file \fBOUT\fR (\fB\-\fR = standard output). Zones come in
\fBdomains.id\fR order and the HELLO of a dump carries pid 0, so two runs on one
database state write the same bytes, whatever the number of
\fB\-\-loader\-threads\fR. It reads the database in one snapshot and does not
start a server. The output file is removed on an error.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-check\fR
Check mode, what \fBkaricheck db\fR runs. It reads the same tables through the
same code as the production load, sends nothing, writes nothing to the database,
and prints what is wrong or odd as lines of the check protocol on standard
output (the schema, every zone with the checks of \fBkaricheck zone\fR, the
settings texts, the views a zone is mapped to). Run it as the user the loader
runs as; its output is meant to be read by \fBkaricheck\fR. No \fB\-\-dump\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-state\-file PATH\fR
With \fB\-s\fR (started by \fBkaridns\fR when \fBloader\-state\-file\fR is set):
the file the loader keeps the hot list in (D1a step 7). The loader reads it at
its start, sends its zones to the Backend as \fBWARM_LIST\fR after the startup
load, and writes it again (\fBPATH.tmp\fR, mode 0600, \fBfsync\fR, \fBrename\fR)
when the Backend sends a \fBHOT_LIST\fR, merged with what it had. A file that is
missing is the first start; one that is empty, truncated, damaged (the last line
is \fBEND <count> <checksum>\fR) or larger than 64 MiB is ignored with a log
line and replaced by the next list. The loader user needs write permission on
the \fBdirectory\fR of the file. The Backend never names this path to the loader
(it comes from the command line the supervisor builds from \fBkaridns.conf\fR),
so a compromised Backend cannot make the loader write anywhere. With
\fB\-\-check\fR: check that the loader can use it (the directory exists and can
be written, the file can be read and is valid); \fBkaricheck db\fR passes it.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-drivers\fR
Print the compiled-in drivers, one per line, and exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-s FD\fR
Stream mode: \fBFD\fR is the socket to the Backend. Started by
\fBkaridns\fR. The loader ends when the Backend closes the stream or on
\fBSIGTERM\fR / \fBSIGINT\fR (also in the middle of a long load: it stops at the
next zone, within milliseconds); \fBSIGHUP\fR is ignored.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-log\-stderr\fR
With \fB\-s\fR: write the messages to standard error instead of syslog, one line
each, \fB<N>[DB] text\fR with \fBN\fR the syslog priority (0 .. 7) and a newline
at the end, at most 500 bytes (a longer text is cut and ends in \fB...\fR;
control characters become \fB?\fR). \fBkaridns\fR starts the loader this way,
with a pipe as its standard error, reads the lines and logs them in the
\fBdatabase\fR category (see MESSAGES). Since 0.7 this includes the messages of
the code the loader shares with the server (the zone checks, such as \fB[Zone]
example.com.: using NSEC3PARAM ...\fR), which went to syslog before: the line is
\fB<N>[DB] [Zone] text\fR. Without this option \fB\-s\fR logs to syslog
(identity \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR, \fB[DB]\fR in front of the text). If standard
error cannot be written (the pipe is closed), the message goes to syslog. It is
an error without \fB\-s\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-debug N\fR
The debug level, 0 .. 99 (default 0): the debug messages up to it are written
(0.7).  \fBkaridns\fR passes the level in force when it starts the loader
(\fBkaridns \-d\fR, \fBkarictl trace\fR), so a loader started again while the
level is raised comes up with it, and in stream mode the Backend tells the
loader every later change (the KDB1 message \fBDEBUG\fR).  With
\fB\-\-log\-stderr\fR a debug message is the line \fB<7:L>[DB] text\fR, \fBL\fR
its level; without it, syslog priority \fBdebug\fR; with \fB\-\-dump\fR a line
on standard error; \fB\-\-check\fR writes none.  At level 1 the loader says when
it connected (driver, file named), its level changes and its batches; at level 2
also each zone it sent (serial, size, a summary or for a fetch) or left out
because the Backend has its version, each fetch request of the Backend (queued,
answered busy or refused because too many wait) and a connection used again.  No
debug message contains a password, the content of a conninfo file or a driver's
error text (which can repeat parts of the connection string): the files are
named, never read out.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-driver DRIVER\fR
\fBsqlite3\fR or \fBpostgresql\fR (a driver that was compiled in).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-database\-file FILE\fR
SQLite3: the database file; it must be in WAL mode. The name is used byte for
byte (\fB%XX\fR is not decoded); a name that starts with \fBfile:\fR is refused
(write \fB./file:...\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-conninfo\-file FILE\fR
PostgreSQL: the file that holds the libpq connection string (owned by the user
that runs the loader, mode 0600 or 0400; its content is never logged).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-schema NAME\fR
PostgreSQL: the schema that holds the tables (default: the \fBsearch_path\fR of
the role).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-view\-index N\fR
The view index in ZONE_BEGIN / ZONE_FAILED, 0..65535, default 0.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-zone NAME\fR
Dump only this zone, read with one query per zone instead of the bulk scan.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-default\-ttl N\fR
TTL for a \fBrecords.ttl\fR that is NULL, 1..2147483647, default 3600.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-loader\-threads N\fR
1..16, default 1. N workers, each with its own database connection, read and
convert disjoint sets of zones (consecutive zones in \fBdomains.id\fR order);
the output is written in \fBdomains.id\fR order whatever N is. The workers take
their snapshots after the main thread took its own (the zone list, the metadata
and the keys); with SQLite3 each of them also reads the change log position of
its snapshot, and if a commit came in between (a position other than the main
thread's) the source is read again from the start, before anything of it is
sent, up to three readings (the last one goes on and logs that a zone can be a
mix of two commits until the next reload). PostgreSQL does not make this check
(its change log cursor is the xmin of the snapshot). In stream mode the number
comes from the configuration (\fBloader\-threads\fR). The fetch stream (the
zones the Backend asks for) is served by a pool of the same size: one thread
reads the requests, and up to N workers (started when a request finds none idle)
read one zone each on a connection of their own; the messages of one zone go out
together, so zones of two workers never mix. Before, one thread read the zones
one at a time (about 500 a second at 11 records a zone in SQLite3).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-map\-view NAME\fR
Keep only the zones that \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR maps to the view \fBNAME\fR
(a domain without a row is kept): what a view that uses the shared source of a
server gets.
.TP 8
\fB\-s FD\fR, \fB\-\-fetch\-fd FD\fR
Stream mode, what \fBkaridns\fR runs: \fBFD\fR is the socket to the Backend
(descriptor 3). \fB\-\-fetch\-fd FD\fR (descriptor 4) is the second stream, for
fetches (below); without it the loader works as before.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-auto\-reload\fR, \fB\-\-server\-id ID\fR, \fB\-\-ecs\-tag NAME\fR, \fB\-\-location\-tag NAME\fR, \fB\-\-max\-zone\-records N\fR, \fB\-\-max\-zone\-bytes N\fR
With \fB\-\-check\fR only: the values of the configuration that change what is
an error (change log triggers are required with auto reload; \fBserver\-id\fR of
a PostgreSQL view; the tags of \fBoptions { ecs\-tags }\fR /
\fBlocation\-tags\fR records may name; the limits of the database
block). \fBkaricheck db\fR passes them from \fBkaridns.conf\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-version\fR, \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the version, or the usage text.
.SH THE FETCH STREAM
D1a (milestone 2). \fBkaridns\fR starts the loader with a second socket,
descriptor 4, next to the stream on descriptor 3 (\fB\-s 3 \-\-fetch\-fd 4\fR);
a restart makes a new pair of each and hands the Backend's two ends over
together. It is a second KDB1 stream (see \fBdns_db_stream.h\fR) for the one
thing that must not wait behind a batch: the Backend asks for one zone because a
parked query waits for it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRequests.\fR \fBFETCH_REQ\fR (request id, view, zone, priority 0 query / 1
warm-up / 2 background, mode 0 the zone / 1 its summary; an optional range,
which this loader refuses). A thread of the loader reads them into a queue of
256, takes the most urgent first (the oldest within a priority) and reads the
zone through the same code as a one-zone reload: its own connection and
snapshot, no batch, no inventory, no results. A request beyond the queue is
answered \fBZONE_FAILED\fR "busy" (the first eight; more are dropped and the
Backend's own time-out ends them).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAnswers.\fR \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR .. \fBZONE_DATA\fR .. \fBZONE_END\fR with the
request id in \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR (a secondary or catalog zone has no data, as in a
batch); or the \fBsummary\fR (mode 1, primary zones): \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR with the
summary flag and \fBZONE_END\fR, carrying the serial, the SOA refresh / retry /
expire and whether the zone is presigned (metadata \fBPRESIGNED=1\fR), no
records, a zero digest. The summary of a zone with no location tags or tinydns
attributes also carries its \fBapex\fR (ZONE_BEGIN tags 15 to 21; the same in
the summaries of a batch): the SOA's names, minimum and TTL, the NS targets (at
most 8, the lowest TTL of the RRset) and the A / AAAA records of the NS targets
that are inside the zone (at most 8 for a name, in the order of the zone, the
lowest TTL of the records of one type), read with \fBscan_apex\fR; a zone with
more than that sends no apex. With them goes tag 22, the \fBNOTIFY addresses\fR
(D1a step 7): the A and AAAA records, in \fBany zone of the same source\fR,
whose name is an NS target that is \fIoutside\fR the zone (found by name,
\fBrecords.name = lower(<NS target>)\fR: an equality on the name index, one
lookup per NS record; PowerDNS stores names in lower case, a name stored with
capitals is not found), at most 8 for one target and 1152 bytes in all (the
first ones in \fBrecords.id\fR order; more do not take the apex away, they are
cut), as records of the NS index in tag 20, the type and the address. The
Backend keeps them with the apex only for a zone that sends NOTIFY to its NS
hosts (\fBnotify yes\fR or \fBprimary\-only\fR) and sends NOTIFY to them when
the zone changes while its data is out of memory (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "Zone
transfers" and "NOTIFY"); it checks the list again (format, the index of a name
outside the zone). A loader that does not send the tag costs the NOTIFY to those
hosts, nothing else. The Backend answers an SOA question for a zone that is out
of memory from it (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "SOA questions for a zone that is out of
memory"). After a reload that makes a zone keep an apex, the Backend asks for
the summary of the zones out of memory that have none (mode 1, priority
warm-up). Or \fBZONE_FAILED\fR with the request id and the reason (the zone is
not in the database, its type is not supported, no SOA, the database could not
be read, busy). Never silence. The Backend's state machine for this stream
(\fBkdb_sm_init_fetch()\fR) accepts nothing else, and discards a zone it did not
ask for.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe end of the stream.\fR The fetch stream is as vital as the first one: when
the Backend closes it (it does after a protocol error of the loader, a full send
buffer or an out-of-memory) or it breaks, the loader ends with status 3 and the
supervisor starts another one with a new pair of streams (after the usual 1, 2,
4 ... 60 s). A loader that went on without a fetch stream would fail every later
fetch "not connected" until it died for some other reason.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBInventory.\fR The Backend's \fBINVENTORY\fR entries have a \fBresident\fR
flag (0 for a zone whose data was taken out of memory or left out because of
\fBmemory\-limit\fR: the entry then carries the serial and no digest). A zone
that is not resident is not sent by a full comparison (no digest to compare); a
zone that changed still is.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot in this milestone.\fR The settings text and the addresses of name servers
outside the zone in the summary (design 7), and a request for a range of a zone.
.SH THE HOT LIST AND THE STATE FILE
D1a step 7. With \fB\-\-state\-file\fR the loader keeps, for the Backend, the
list of the zones that are asked for most (see \fBkaridns.conf\fR,
\fBloader\-state\-file\fR, \fBhot\-list\-size\fR, \fBhot\-list\-interval\fR and
\fBwarm\-before\-serve\fR). Two messages on the first stream carry it, both in
parts (at most 16384 zones and 1 MiB each; the zones are named by view name and
zone name, because the file outlives a reconfiguration):
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBHOT_LIST\fB\fR (Backend to loader): the zones most asked for, hottest
first, with the size the file may have. The loader checks it as it checks
everything the Backend sends: at most 100000 zones, the parts in order (a part
that does not follow drops the list, not the stream), a view that its CONFIG
does not have is dropped, the names are canonical (the stream decoder refuses
anything else). When the last part has come the loader merges it into the list
of the file (the new zones first, then the zones of the old list that are not in
the new one, up to the size), writes the file if the list changed, and answers
\fB\fBHOT_ACK\fB\fR (written, unchanged, failed with the reason, or no file
configured). A HOT_ACK is sent between two batches, never inside one.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBWARM_LIST\fB\fR (loader to Backend): once, after the startup load, the
zones of the file in order (an empty list when the file is missing or damaged),
with what the loader found in the file at its start (\fBloaded\fR,
\fBmissing\fR, \fBcorrupt\fR, \fBunreadable\fR) for \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR.
.PP
The file is text: \fBKARIDNS\-HOT\-LIST 1\fR, one line \fBview<TAB>zone\fR for
each zone, hottest first, and \fBEND <count> <checksum>\fR (FNV-1a over the
entry lines, eight hex digits). \fBPermissions.\fR The loader runs as
\fBloader\-user\fR and writes in the directory of the file: give it a directory
of its own, owned by \fBloader\-user\fR, mode 0700 (\fBinstall \-d \-o
karidns\-db \-m 0700 /var/db/karidns/state\fR). The file is created with mode
0600. The Backend, in capability mode, never opens it. The loader opens the file
with \fBO_NOFOLLOW\fR, so \fBa state file that is a symbolic link is
UNREADABLE\fR (\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows
\fBstate\-file: ... at_start=unreadable, ignored\fR), even when the link points
to a good file: give \fBloader\-state\-file\fR the real path (a link in a
directory \fIabove\fR the file is fine; only the last component counts). A state
file that exists and cannot be read (permissions, a link, not a regular file) is
\fBnot replaced\fR: the loader says so in its log and in the acknowledgement of
every hot list (\fBfailed\fR), and keeps going without a state file until it is
fixed or removed. The count and checksum of the \fBEND\fR line must have exactly
the form written (decimal digits, one space, eight lower case hex digits); the
limit of a list is the one its first part says. The file is not trusted when it
is read: it may be damaged, or written by somebody who could write in the
directory; whatever its contents, the Backend reads only zones it has in its
directory, at most 100000 of them, at the rate of \fBfetch\-rate\fR.
.SH MESSAGES
Run on its own (\fB\-\-dump\fR, \fB\-\-check\fR), the loader writes its messages
to standard error, \fBkaridns\-dbloader: text\fR, as it always did (the dump
tests and scripts read those lines).
.PP
Started by \fBkaridns\fR (\fB\-s\fR), the loader's standard error is a pipe that
the supervisor (the manager process, which also restarts the loader) creates for
every start: \fBkaridns\-dbloader \-s 3 [\-\-fetch\-fd 4] [\-\-state\-file PATH]
\-\-log\-stderr\fR. The loader writes \fB<N>[DB] text\fR lines; the supervisor
reads the pipe without blocking and logs each line in the \fBdatabase\fR
category of BIND's \fBlogging { }\fR statement, with the severity of \fBN\fR
(\fB<3>\fR error, \fB<4>\fR warning, \fB<5>\fR notice, \fB<6>\fR info) and, for
a debug message (0.7, \fB\-\-debug\fR), \fB<7:L>\fR with \fBL\fR its debug
level, 1 .. 99, logged at \fBdebug L\fR (the loader writes levels 1 and 2; an
\fBL\fR above 10, the highest level of the server's own messages, is logged at
\fBdebug 10\fR; a channel with \fBseverity dynamic\fR writes it while the debug
level in force is at least \fBL\fR, \fBseverity debug <n>\fR when \fBn\fR is at
least \fBL\fR; the text is cut at 255 bytes), so a configuration such as
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
logging {
    channel dbfile { file "/var/log/karidns/database.log" versions 5 size 10m; severity info; print\-time yes; print\-severity yes; print\-category yes; };
    category database { dbfile; };
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
collects the messages of the loader together with the \fB[DB]\fR messages of the
Backend (stream, batches, installs). Example lines:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:56.789 database: warning: [DB] zone 'zero.example.test.' (domains.id 2): the SOA serial is 0; PowerDNS may compute the served serial (SOA\-EDIT, auto\-serial), but KariDNS uses the stored value as it is, so secondaries and NOTIFY see serial 0
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:56.790 database: info: [DB] state file /var/db/karidns/hot.list: 12 zone(s) of the hot list
28\-Oct\-2026 12:35:10.001 database: warning: [DB] 480 line(s) of the loader's output were not logged (more than 50 per second)
.fi
.RE
.PP
A message is logged once: when the loader is supervised it does not also write
to syslog (syslog is only the fallback when the pipe cannot be written), and
with no \fBcategory database\fR the lines go to the channels of \fBdefault\fR /
\fBdefault_syslog\fR like any other category. The bytes of the pipe are
untrusted input of the manager, which runs with root's rights until it drops
them:
.IP \(bu 2
a line is cut at 512 bytes of text and ends in \fB...\fR; the rest of a longer
line is read and thrown away, never kept;
.IP \(bu 2
everything but printable ASCII is written as \fB\exNN\fR (a NUL, ESC, a CR that
does not end a CRLF, bytes of 0x80 and above), a tab as a space, so that nothing
a database name contains can start a line of the log or hide in it;
.IP \(bu 2
a line without \fB<N>\fR (or \fB<7:L>\fR with \fBL\fR 1 .. 99 written without a
leading 0) is logged at \fBnotice\fR with the text as it is, so a line of
another version is never lost; empty lines are dropped; the end of the stream or
the loader's end logs a last line that has no newline;
.IP \(bu 2
at most 50 lines per second of \fBwarning\fR and worse are logged, and at most
50 of the others (notice, info, debug: the shared zone code says one info line
per presigned zone, and such a flood must not drop an error of the same second);
the others are counted and reported by one \fBwarning\fR line when their second
is over (also when the loader writes nothing more);
.IP \(bu 2
one read takes at most 64 KiB, and the manager looks at its signals between
reads, so a loader that floods the pipe cannot keep the manager from
\fBSIGTERM\fR; a full pipe makes the loader wait;
.IP \(bu 2
the pipe is made for every start (close-on-exec, read end non-blocking), drained
when the loader ends and then closed: restarts do not leave descriptors behind.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
Every zone was written (\fB\-\-dump\fR), or nothing wrong was found
(\fB\-\-check\fR).
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
The stream is complete but some zones went out as ZONE_FAILED (the reason is on
standard error), or \fB\-\-check\fR found errors.
.TP 8
\fB2\fR
Usage error (a bad option or option value, a stray argument, a missing or
contradicting option; the usage text or the reason goes to standard error);
nothing was read. \fB\-h\fR / \fB\-\-help\fR print the usage text to standard
output and exit with 0. The other tools use 2 for usage errors too
(\fBkaricheck\fR, \fBkarictl\fR, \fBkaridns\fR).
.TP 8
\fB3\fR
Error: database, schema, output or memory.
.TP 8
\fB4\fR
Transient database error (busy); try again.
.SH SCHEMA AND TRIGGER FILES
The loader reads the tables of the \fBPowerDNS 5.1 generic SQLite3 / PostgreSQL
backend\fR (\fBdomains\fR, \fBrecords\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR,
\fBcryptokeys\fR; the PowerDNS schema file is not part of KariDNS: take
\fBschema.sqlite3.sql\fR or \fBschema.pgsql.sql\fR from PowerDNS). KariDNS adds
its own tables and triggers in the files below. The extension is optional;
without it a database still loads, but there is no change log (zones are
compared by SOA serial only), no view mapping, no profiles and no tags, and
\fBkaricheck db\fR says what that costs.
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR
SQLite3: schema version 3 (version 2 plus the tables \fBkaridns_audit_log\fR and
\fBkaridns_audit_actor\fR, and \fBkaridns_key_requests\fR and
\fBkaridns_signer_state\fR of the signer, which the loader does not use; the
audit triggers are in \fBdb/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR, applied when the
WebAdmin or the signer is used, and the users, groups and grants of the WebAdmin
in the file \fBdb/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR, which the API ATTACHes: the
loader never opens it). \fBkaridns_changes\fR (the change log: which domain
changed, written by triggers on \fBdomains\fR, \fBrecords\fR,
\fBdomainmetadata\fR, \fBcryptokeys\fR and the tables below), a trigger that
keeps the newest 1,000,000 log rows, \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR,
\fBkaridns_profiles\fR, \fBkaridns_record_tags\fR, \fBkaridns_record_tinydns\fR,
\fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR, \fBkaridns_schema_version\fR. Apply it once, as the
owner of the database, after the PowerDNS schema. It can be applied again.
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR
PostgreSQL: schema version 3 (version 2 plus the schema \fBkaridns_auth\fR:
users, groups, grants, the audit log, the RLS functions and policies; see THE
AUTH SCHEMA, ROLES AND THE AUDIT LOG below). The same tables with
statement-level triggers (one log row per distinct domain per statement),
\fBNOTIFY karidns_changes\fR for auto reload, and the multi-server tables
\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR and \fBkaridns_server_status\fR. PostgreSQL 13 or
newer. Apply it in the schema that holds the PowerDNS tables.
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_ext_v1_to_v2.sqlite3.sql\fR, \fBdb/karidns_ext_v1_to_v2.pgsql.sql\fR
Convert a database from schema version 1 (the extension files of the early
betas) to version 2. The loader reads both versions and serves a version 1
database without a change log.
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR
PostgreSQL: the privileges of the role that \fBkaridns\-zone\fR imports with
(\fBpsql \-v role=... \-v schema=...\fR). \fBSELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE\fR
on \fBdomains\fR, \fBrecords\fR and \fBdomainmetadata\fR, the \fBkaridns_*\fR
zone tables, what the change-log triggers need, and only the column privilege
\fBSELECT (domain_id)\fR on \fBcryptokeys\fR (never \fBcontent\fR, the private
keys). Not for the loader: give the loader \fBkaridns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR.
.PP
A bulk import writes one change-log row per changed row (SQLite3) or per
statement (PostgreSQL); a big import makes the next reload compare every zone
once. The files carry their own documentation in their first lines.
.PP
The users, groups, grants, database roles, row level security and the audit log
of the WebAdmin and the signer (the schema \fBkaridns_auth\fR,
\fBdb/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql\fR, \fBdb/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR,
\fBdb/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR, \fBdb/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR) are
described in THE AUTH SCHEMA, ROLES AND THE AUDIT LOG, below.
.SS Indexes
The extension files create \fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs_domain_idx\fR on
\fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs (domain_id)\fR (the loader reads the tag definitions of
one zone at a time; without the index every such read scans the table). It was
added after schema version 2, which stays 2: apply \fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR
(or the \fBv1_to_v2\fR script) again to get it on an existing database.
.PP
\fBThe loader is read-only and never creates an index.\fR Two optional
PostgreSQL indexes on the PowerDNS tables make the big reads faster; create them
yourself when the database is large:
.TP 8
\fBCREATE INDEX records_soa_cover_idx ON records (domain_id) INCLUDE (name, content) WHERE type = 'SOA' AND disabled = false;\fR
The zone list of every full comparison (\fBkarictl reload\fR, auto reload, a
loader restart) joins each domain to its apex SOA. With this partial covering
index the join is an index-only merge over one row per zone. The loader looks
the index up once per connection (\fBpg_indexes\fR) and then leaves the
planner's \fBcursor_tuple_fraction\fR at its default (0.1) for the list; without
the index it plans the list for all of its rows (\fB1\fR), which is then the
faster plan. Measured at 1,000,000 zones x 11 records (\fBpsql\fR, 3 runs):
without the index 2.2 s planned for all rows (one pass over the SOA rows)
against 2.6 s at the default (a walk of the whole \fBrecords\fR table through
its \fBdomain_id\fR index); with the index 1.7 s planned for all rows (the
planner still reads every SOA row) against 1.0 s at the default (the index-only
merge).
.TP 8
\fBCREATE INDEX domains_lower_name_idx ON domains (lower(name));\fR
A one-zone read of a zone that is not in the database (a deletion, a typo in
\fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR) falls back to \fBlower(d.name)\fR, a table scan of
\fBdomains\fR without this expression index (about 0.2 s at 1,000,000 zones).
.PP
\fBMigration note: \fBrecords.name\fB of address rows must be in lower case.\fR
When a zone is out of memory (\fBmemory\-limit\fR), the Backend sends its NOTIFY
from the zone's summary, and the summary carries the addresses of the NS targets
that are \fIoutside\fR the zone (tag 22 of \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR,
\fBKDB_NOTIFY_ADDR_PER_NS\fR per name). The loader reads them with \fBg.name =
lower(<NS target>)\fR (SQLite3 and PostgreSQL alike), an exact comparison that
the index on \fBrecords (name)\fR serves; it does not fold the case of
\fBrecords.name\fR. An A / AAAA row of such a target whose \fBname\fR has an
upper case letter is not found, and that name server gets no NOTIFY while the
zone is out of memory (the addresses \fIinside\fR the zone are matched
case-insensitively and are not affected). PowerDNS stores names in lower case,
so a database written by PowerDNS or by \fBzone2sql\fR is fine; before pointing
the loader at data from another source or edited by hand, check \fBSELECT id,
name FROM records WHERE type IN ('A', 'AAAA') AND name <> lower(name);\fR and
fix the rows (\fBUPDATE records SET name = lower(name) WHERE name <>
lower(name);\fR).
.PP
\fBZone names that differ only in case are not supported.\fR PowerDNS stores
\fBdomains.name\fR in lower case, and so should the data: two rows of one source
such as \fBExample.test\fR and \fBexample.test\fR are two rows for PostgreSQL's
case-sensitive unique index (the PowerDNS PostgreSQL schema has a \fBCHECK\fR
constraint \fBc_lowercase_name\fR that refuses upper case, and SQLite3's schema
declares the column \fBCOLLATE NOCASE\fR; such rows only exist when a constraint
was dropped or the data was written around it). The loader keeps the one-zone
lookup (\fB\-\-zone\fR, used by reload requests and auto reload) fast: it
matches the indexed name exactly first and falls back to \fBlower()\fR only when
that finds nothing. With such duplicates a full read and a one-zone read may
therefore pick different rows. \fBkaricheck db\fR (\fBkaridns\-dbloader
\-\-check\fR) warns about every pair of \fBdomains\fR rows of one source whose
names are equal ignoring case, with both \fBdomains.id\fR values (both drivers);
fix the data, do not rely on either row.
.SH THE AUTH SCHEMA, ROLES AND THE AUDIT LOG (0.6.0)
Schema version 3 adds what the WebAdmin API and the signer daemon need: users,
groups and grants, database roles held to their grants by row level security
(RLS, PostgreSQL only), an audit log, and the signer's two tables. The server
and \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR use none of it (but for the metadata
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR, below). A database that only has the loader's and the
importer's roles behaves as it did before: RLS is off and nothing is logged.
.PP
\fBThe signer's tables (0.6.0 item C).\fR The daemon that uses them is
\fBkaridns\-signer(8)\fR. \fBkaridns_key_requests\fR and
\fBkaridns_signer_state\fR are in \fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR and
\fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR (per data schema, like the other
\fBkaridns_*\fR tables; apply the file again to a version 3 database that lacks
them; the schema version stays 3). They go away with their zone (\fBON DELETE
CASCADE\fR), are covered by the audit (\fBenable_audit\fR; the SQLite3 audit
file has their triggers) and write \fBno\fR row to \fBkaridns_changes\fR: the
signer writes its state after every run, and a signer that followed its own
state would never stop.
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_key_requests\fR
\fBid\fR, \fBdomain_id\fR, \fBkind\fR (\fBenable\fR = start signing the zone:
generate the first key or keys, set the metadata; \fBadd\fR = one more key),
\fBalgorithm\fR (8, 10, 13, 14, 15, 16; default 13), \fBbits\fR (RSA only,
1024..8192), \fBkey_type\fR (\fBcsk\fR, \fBksk\fR, \fBzsk\fR; default
\fBcsk\fR), \fBnsec\fR (\fBnsec3\fR, \fBnsec\fR; \fBenable\fR only), \fBstate\fR
(\fBpending\fR, \fBdone\fR, \fBfailed\fR), \fBmessage\fR, \fBrequested_by\fR (a
principal id), \fBcreated_at\fR, \fBdone_at\fR (set when the state is not
\fBpending\fR). The WebAdmin (a global master or the domain master of the zone)
or an operator files a row; the signer answers it. A unique partial index
(\fBkaridns_key_requests_one_pending_idx\fR) allows \fBone pending request per
zone\fR: a second one is refused until the first is answered. On PostgreSQL the
WebAdmin's role may \fBINSERT\fR only the columns \fBdomain_id\fR, \fBkind\fR,
\fBalgorithm\fR, \fBbits\fR, \fBkey_type\fR, \fBnsec\fR and \fBrequested_by\fR
(not the state, the message, \fBdone_at\fR or the id), and the signer's role may
\fBUPDATE\fR only \fBstate\fR, \fBmessage\fR and \fBdone_at\fR.
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_signer_state\fR
\fBdomain_id\fR (the key), \fBsigned_at\fR, \fBsigned_serial\fR,
\fBmin_expire\fR (the earliest RRSIG expiration of the zone, as \fBkaridns\-zone
import \-\-signer\fR reports it), \fBnext_due\fR (when the signer wants to look
at the zone again; indexed), \fBattempts\fR (failed runs since the last
success), \fBlast_error\fR, \fBlast_duration_ms\fR. Only the signer writes it.
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_signer_monitor\fR (a view)
one row per zone with \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = 1 (0.6.1): \fBdomain_id\fR,
\fBzone\fR, the columns of \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR (empty for a zone without
a state row), \fBstatus\fR (the first that applies of \fBexpired\fR,
\fBfailing\fR, \fBoverdue\fR (\fBnext_due\fR over an hour ago), \fBexpiring\fR
(signatures end within two days), \fBunsigned\fR, \fBok\fR) and
\fBseconds_to_expiry\fR. For a monitoring system: \fBkaridns\-signer(8)\fR,
"Monitoring". PostgreSQL: it is the owner's and does not apply the reader's row
level security; grant \fBSELECT\fR on the view to the monitoring role and on
nothing else.
.PP
PostgreSQL stores the times as \fBtimestamptz\fR; SQLite3 as unix seconds
(\fBINTEGER\fR), like \fBkaridns_changes.changed_at\fR.
.PP
\fBThe zones of the signer (\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fB).\fR \fBkaridns\-zone
import \-\-signer\fR writes the metadata \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = \fB1\fR on a
zone the signer keeps signed. The server and the loader know it in one place:
with \fBdatabase { signer\-wait yes; }\fR (the default) the loader does not
reload such a zone because of the change log or a moved serial, only on a reload
request or in a full comparison, so a change that is not signed yet is not
served (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, "The signer's zones"). \fBPowerDNS\fR on the same
database reads it live and serves a change at once, signed or not: there the
zone is only as safe as the order of the writes; keep PowerDNS and the signer
apart, or let the signer be the only writer of a signed zone's DNSSEC records.
.SS What version 3 adds
.TP 8
PostgreSQL (\fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR)
The schema \fBkaridns_auth\fR with the tables \fBprincipals\fR,
\fBcredentials\fR, \fBmemberships\fR, \fBprincipal_closure\fR,
\fBrr_policies\fR, \fBgrants\fR, \fBaudit_log\fR and \fBschema_version\fR; the
functions and triggers that keep them consistent; the RLS policies for the
tables of the data schema (they do nothing while RLS is off); and the audit
triggers of \fBkaridns_auth\fR itself. There is one \fBkaridns_auth\fR for the
whole database, shared by every data schema (a view that has a schema of its own
applies the same file there and adds its own triggers and policies).
.TP 8
SQLite3
\fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR adds the tables \fBkaridns_audit_log\fR and
\fBkaridns_audit_actor\fR to the zone file. The users, groups and grants live in
a file of their own, \fBdb/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR, so that the loader,
which only opens the zone file, can never read a password hash. SQLite3 has no
roles and no RLS: the API does the checks that PostgreSQL does in the policies.
.PP
Items D and E (the API, templates, zone versions) add tables and functions to
version 3 in the same way (below, "The API tables, the zone generation and
versions, and the check role"). Both: the version row says 3
(\fBkaridns_auth.schema_version\fR and \fBkaridns_schema_version\fR). The loader
and \fBkaricheck db\fR read versions 1, 2 and 3. There is no v2-to-v3 file:
apply the version 3 file again and the database becomes version 3 (it only adds
objects). Version 1 needs the \fBv1_to_v2\fR file first.
.SS Files and the order to apply them
.IP 1. 4
The PowerDNS schema (from PowerDNS, not part of KariDNS).
.IP 2. 4
PostgreSQL: \fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR, as the owner of the PowerDNS tables,
who needs \fBCREATE\fR on the database (for \fBCREATE SCHEMA
karidns_auth\fR). Once per data schema; it can be applied again. SQLite3:
\fBsqlite3 zones.sqlite3 < db/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR, and \fBsqlite3
auth.sqlite3 < db/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR as the user of the API.
.IP 3. 4
The roles of the tools, as an administrator, once per data schema:
\fBdb/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR (the server's loader),
\fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR (\fBkaridns\-zone\fR),
\fBdb/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql\fR (the WebAdmin API),
\fBdb/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR (the signer). They can be run in any
order and again.
.IP 4. 4
\fBGRANT karidns_rls_trusted TO <the PowerDNS gpgsql role>;\fR and the same for
every other role that must see everything (backup, monitoring). The loader and
writer scripts do it for their role.
.IP 5. 4
SQLite3 only, when the API or the signer is used: \fBsqlite3 zones.sqlite3 <
db/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR (the audit triggers).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-f db/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql karidns
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_api    \-v schema=public \-f db/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql karidns
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_signer \-v schema=public \-f db/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql karidns
.fi
.RE
.PP
The ext file only creates things. RLS and the audit triggers on the PowerDNS
tables are switched on by the API and signer role scripts
(\fBkaridns_auth.enable_rls()\fR, \fBenable_audit()\fR); switching RLS on in the
ext file would hide every row from the loader, from \fBkaridns\-zone\fR and from
PowerDNS itself. \fBkaridns_auth.disable_rls('public')\fR and
\fBdisable_audit('public')\fR switch them off again.
.SS Users, groups and tenants
A \fBprincipal\fR is a user or a group (\fBprincipals.kind\fR). Names are unique
ignoring case. A \fBuser\fR may have \fBcredentials\fR (a password hash as a PHC
string, \fB$argon2id$\fR or \fB$scrypt$\fR: the database checks the prefix only;
an opaque TOTP secret, which the API should store encrypted with a key of its
own; an OIDC issuer and subject; failed-login and lock fields). Only the API
role reads the credentials, and only a user's own row and, for a global master,
all of them.
.PP
A \fBmembership\fR puts a user or a group into a group. Groups nest, at most 8
deep; a cycle is refused, and so is a user as a group. \fBprincipal_closure\fR
lists every principal with all the groups it is in, directly or not (plus itself
at depth 0); triggers keep it, so a policy joins it instead of recursing. A
group that is in no group is a \fBtenant\fR; a principal's tenants are the top
groups of its closure. The database only stores this; the API uses it ("grant
only inside my tenant", lists). A disabled principal, or a grant held through a
disabled group, counts for nothing.
.SS Record policies (rr_policies)
A grant carries a record policy: \fBallowed_types\fR (a list; never a
DNSSEC-managed type: \fBRRSIG\fR, \fBNSEC\fR, \fBNSEC3\fR, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR,
\fBDNSKEY\fR, \fBCDS\fR, \fBCDNSKEY\fR; \fBDS\fR is an ordinary type),
\fBallow_wildcard\fR, \fBallow_delegation\fR (\fBNS\fR below the apex),
\fBallow_dname\fR, \fBapex_editable\fR, \fBttl_min\fR and \fBttl_max\fR, and
\fBmax_rr\fR. All the flags default to false. The database enforces everything
but \fBmax_rr\fR, which the API checks.
.SS Grants
A grant gives a principal a role on zones. The zone is named by \fB(data schema,
zone name)\fR, not by \fBdomains.id\fR: there is one \fBkaridns_auth\fR and
several data schemas, and in SQLite3 the grants are in another file. Names are
stored lower case without the final dot.
.TP 8
\fBglobal_master\fR
Every zone of the database. Its record policy still limits the records it may
write.
.TP 8
\fBdomain_master\fR
One zone: all its records that the record policy allows.
.TP 8
\fBsubdomain_master\fR
One zone, and only the names at or below a suffix inside it (at a label
boundary: \fBevilsub.example.com\fR is not under \fBsub.example.com\fR). It may
not change the apex.
.PP
The principal's effective grants are its own and those of every group it is
in. A trigger on \fBdomains\fR removes the grants of a zone that is deleted and
renames them when the zone is renamed, so a grant never moves to a new zone of
the same name that someone else creates later. On SQLite3 the API does this
cleanup itself (the grants are in another file).
.PP
\fBNo escalation.\fR A trigger on \fBkaridns_auth.grants\fR binds the sessions
of the API role (and of every role that is a member of it; the owner and other
roles are the operator and are not bound). A global master may grant anything. A
domain master may grant \fBdomain_master\fR or \fBsubdomain_master\fR on its own
zone, with a record policy that is a \fBsubset\fR of the one it holds (fewer
types, no flag it lacks, a TTL range inside its own, \fBmax_rr\fR not higher; no
limit counts as the highest). A subdomain master may not grant. A grant may be
deleted by a global master, or by a domain master of the zone for a grant it
could have made. \fBgranted_by\fR and \fBgranted_at\fR are set by the trigger. A
record policy that a global master changes later is trusted.
.SS Roles (PostgreSQL)
The role scripts take \fB\-v role=\fR and \fB\-v schema=\fR, so a site can name
its roles. Each script revokes every earlier privilege of its role on the tables
of the schema first.
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql\fR
The role the API connects as (created \fBNOLOGIN\fR if it does not exist:
\fBGRANT\fR it to the login role, which must not be a member of
\fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR). \fBSELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE\fR on
\fBdomains\fR, \fBrecords\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR and the \fBkaridns_*\fR zone
tables, only the column privilege \fBSELECT (id, domain_id, flags, active,
published)\fR on \fBcryptokeys\fR (never \fBcontent\fR), the users, groups,
grants and credentials of \fBkaridns_auth\fR (the only role that reads
\fBcredentials\fR), \fBSELECT\fR on the audit log, \fBEXECUTE\fR on
\fBkaridns_auth.bump_serial()\fR. \fBSELECT\fR and \fBINSERT\fR (no
\fBDELETE\fR) on \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR; nothing on
\fBkaridns_changes\fR. No \fBTRUNCATE\fR, no write to the audit log. It switches
RLS and the audit log on. The script stops, before it changes anything, if the
role is a superuser or has \fBBYPASSRLS\fR, is a member of
\fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR, or is, or is a member of, the owner of the tables
(directly or through other roles).
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR
The signer: reads every table including \fBcryptokeys.content\fR and the change
log \fBkaridns_changes\fR (it follows the log to see which zones changed);
inserts and deletes the DNSSEC-managed record types; updates records (a trigger
decides what: below); writes the metadata kinds \fBPRESIGNED\fR,
\fBNSEC3PARAM\fR and \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR; inserts reload requests and new
keys (\fBINSERT\fR on \fBcryptokeys\fR, no \fBUPDATE\fR or \fBDELETE\fR);
answers the key requests (\fBSELECT\fR, and \fBUPDATE\fR of the columns
\fBstate\fR, \fBmessage\fR and \fBdone_at\fR only, on
\fBkaridns_key_requests\fR); keeps its schedule (\fBSELECT\fR, \fBINSERT\fR,
\fBUPDATE\fR, \fBDELETE\fR on \fBkaridns_signer_state\fR). Nothing on
\fBkaridns_auth\fR, nothing written to \fBkaridns_changes\fR. It is a trusted
daemon held to its types by RLS and a trigger, not bound by grants, and its role
is \fBnot\fR in \fBkaridns_auth.audit_restricted_roles\fR (\fBkaridns\-zone
import \-\-signer\fR writes the audit in summary mode: a re-sign replaces every
signature, and a row per signature is a log that nobody reads). Its write
policies of \fBrecords\fR and \fBdomainmetadata\fR exist twice, permissive and
\fBRESTRICTIVE\fR, so setting \fBkaridns.principal\fR to a global master does
not let it write an \fBA\fR record. The trigger on \fBrecords\fR (for the roles
in \fBkaridns_auth.signer_roles\fR) holds it to its columns: a DNSSEC record may
change in any column; \fBevery other record only in \fBordername\fB and
\fBauth\fB\fR (the NSEC3 hash of its owner and the authoritative flag, which a
signature chain needs; the trigger is declared \fBBEFORE UPDATE OF\fR every
column but those two, so it does not fire for an \fBUPDATE\fR of just those two,
like \fBpdnsutil rectify\-zone\fR's, and costs nothing there); an SOA row only
in its serial, \fBordername\fR and \fBauth\fR (type, name, domain, TTL,
priority, the flag and the rest of the content stay), and it neither inserts nor
deletes an SOA. The same refusals as for the API role apply at install time.
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_check_role.pgsql.sql\fR
The check role of the API (items D and E): the writer role's privileges,
membership of \fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR and of \fBkaridns_never_commit\fR,
\fBUSAGE\fR on \fBkaridns_auth\fR and \fBEXECUTE\fR on \fBbump_serial\fR; its
transactions cannot commit a write (below).
.TP 8
\fBdb/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR, \fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR
As before, plus membership in \fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR. Nothing on
\fBkaridns_auth\fR. The loader and \fBkaridns\-zone\fR read and write everything
with RLS on, in either script order.
.TP 8
the owner
Owns everything and is not subject to RLS (no \fBFORCE\fR). The only role that
may \fBUPDATE\fR or \fBDELETE\fR audit rows.
.PP
Nobody gets \fBBYPASSRLS\fR or superuser.
.SS What row level security checks
The API sets \fBSET LOCAL karidns.principal = '<id>'\fR in every transaction;
the policies read it. A missing, empty or non-numeric setting, a principal that
does not exist and a disabled principal are all "no principal": no rows, no
writes.
.PP
A row is \fBwritable\fR when one effective grant of the principal covers it: the
name is in the grant's scope, the type is allowed and not DNSSEC-managed, the
TTL (NULL counts as 0) is in the policy's range, a wildcard label needs
\fBallow_wildcard\fR, \fBNS\fR below the apex needs \fBallow_delegation\fR,
\fBDNAME\fR needs \fBallow_dname\fR, and the apex needs \fBapex_editable\fR and
a role other than subdomain master. Two grants never combine for one row. The
SOA changes only through \fBkaridns_auth.bump_serial(schema, domain_id)\fR (adds
1, RFC 1982; any grant on the zone may call it), or for a global master whose
policy allows it. The type names \fBTYPE2\fR, \fBTYPE6\fR, \fBTYPE39\fR and
\fBTYPE46\fR ... \fBTYPE60\fR are read as the names they stand for, so an alias
gets the rules of the name. A record outside its zone, a row without a name and
(outside the apex) an empty non-terminal follow the same scope rule.
.PP
A row is \fBvisible\fR when a grant of the principal reaches it: a domain master
sees the records of its zone, a subdomain master those of its suffix, a global
master every zone; the SOA and the apex \fBNS\fR are visible to every grant on
the zone. A row without a name, a row whose name is outside its zone and a row
without a zone are invisible to the API, a global master included (the owner
repairs them). A trailing dot of \fBdomains.name\fR is ignored.
.PP
The signer's tables follow the same rule: a global master or the domain master
of the zone files a key request (\fBINSERT\fR into \fBkaridns_key_requests\fR:
state \fBpending\fR, no message, \fBrequested_by\fR the acting principal itself)
and sees the requests of its zones; any grant on the zone reads
\fBkaridns_signer_state\fR; the API cannot change either table (the signer
does).
.PP
The other tables follow the zone: \fBdomains\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR, the tag
tables, the key table (without \fBcontent\fR) and the views are visible to a
grant on the zone; a global master writes \fBdomains\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR
(not the kinds the signer owns) and \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR; a domain master
writes \fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR; the tag rows of a record follow the
record. \fBprincipals\fR, \fBmemberships\fR and \fBrr_policies\fR are readable
by every API session (a login looks the user up by name before it has a
principal id) and changed by a global master. The audit log is visible to a
global master (all of it) and to a domain master (the rows of its zones).
.PP
\fBWhat the API must check itself.\fR The database cannot see: that one grant
allows a whole change set (it checks each row alone), \fBmax_rr\fR, a CNAME
beside other data and the other zone checks (\fBkaricheck \-\-json\fR), and that
a change set stays in one zone.
.SS karidns_rls_trusted
The policies bind every role that is not a member of \fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR,
a \fBNOLOGIN\fR role the scripts create. A role that must see and write
everything joins it: the loader and writer scripts do it for their role, and for
the PowerDNS \fBgpgsql\fR backend's role and any other role you run
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
GRANT karidns_rls_trusted TO <the PowerDNS gpgsql role>;
.fi
.RE
.PP
A role that uses the tables without it is refused (no rows, or \fBpermission
denied for function\fR) once RLS is on. The table owner is not subject to RLS
and is the "operator" for the grant trigger: do not make the owner a member of
the API role. The maintenance triggers of the data tables (the change log
\fBkaridns_changes\fR and its prune, the reload requests and their prune) run as
the owner with a pinned \fBsearch_path\fR, so the API and signer roles need no
right on the change log and cannot read, forge or delete it.
.SS The trust boundary
\fBkaridns.principal\fR is a custom setting that any session can set. The API is
trusted to set it to the logged-in user. RLS catches the API's own mistakes (a
missing \fBWHERE\fR, a wrong zone id); it does not stop a compromised API
process or a stolen database password of the API role. That is why the API role
gets nothing of \fBcryptokeys.content\fR, nothing of what the signer or the
owner may do, and no way to change or delete audit rows. Treat the API's
database password like the key to every zone it has a grant on.
.SS The audit log
With the audit triggers on, every change of the tables of the data schema and of
\fBkaridns_auth\fR is logged. PostgreSQL: \fBkaridns_auth.audit_log\fR; SQLite3:
\fBkaridns_audit_log\fR in the zone file and \fBkaridns_auth_audit\fR in the
auth file. The columns:
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Column	\&Meaning
_
\&\fBid\fR, \fBat\fR	\&A running number, the time.
\&\fBxid\fR	T{
The transaction (PostgreSQL), to group the rows of one commit.
T}
\&\fBdb_role\fR	T{
The database role (the role after \fBSET ROLE\fR, else the login
role). \fBkaridns\-zone\fR on SQLite3: \fBkaridns\-zone:<OS user>\fR.
T}
\&\fBprincipal_id\fR, \fBprincipal_name\fR	T{
\fBkaridns.principal\fR of the session and its name (copied, so it survives the
deletion of the principal). NULL without one.
T}
\&\fBchange_set\fR	T{
\fBkaridns.change_set\fR of the session: the API's change set.
T}
T{
\fBdata_schema\fR, \fBtable_name\fR, \fBop\fR, \fBzone_name\fR, \fBrow_id\fR
T}	T{
Where: the table, \fBI\fR, \fBU\fR or \fBD\fR, the zone, the row's id.
T}
\&\fBbefore\fR, \fBafter\fR	\&The row as JSON.
.TE
.PP
\fBRedaction.\fR \fBcryptokeys.content\fR, \fBcredentials.password_hash\fR and
\fBcredentials.totp_secret\fR are written as \fB"<redacted>"\fR: the API reads
the audit log, and keys and secrets must not reach it.
.PP
\fBSummary mode.\fR A big import writes two rows per record (the old row and the
new one). A role other than the API role may \fBSET LOCAL karidns.audit
= 'summary'\fR (\fBkaridns\-zone import \-\-audit summary\fR does, and \fBimport
\-\-signer\fR does by default): the audit then writes one row per transaction,
table, operation and zone that counts the statements and the rows
(\fB{"summary": true, "statements": 100000, "rows": 100000}\fR), filled in when
the transaction commits. The API role and any role that is a member of it (also
after \fBSET ROLE\fR) cannot turn the detail off; the restricted roles are the
rows of \fBkaridns_auth.audit_restricted_roles\fR, which only the owner
writes. The tables of \fBkaridns_auth\fR are always logged row by row. SQLite3
has no summary mode.
.PP
\fBAppend only.\fR Only the owner has \fBINSERT\fR, \fBUPDATE\fR or \fBDELETE\fR
on the log; the triggers are \fBSECURITY DEFINER\fR and insert as the
owner. Nobody gets \fBTRUNCATE\fR, and a \fBTRUNCATE\fR of an audited table is
not logged (use \fBDELETE\fR).
.PP
\fBPruning.\fR Nothing is pruned automatically. The owner deletes by date:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
DELETE FROM karidns_auth.audit_log WHERE at < now() \- interval '1 year';
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run it in batches on a big log (\fBWHERE id IN (SELECT id ... LIMIT 100000)\fR)
and \fBVACUUM\fR afterwards. At scale, partition the table by month (declare
\fBaudit_log\fR partitioned by \fBat\fR and drop old partitions); that needs a
migration of the table and is planned for 0.7.
.SS SQLite3
.IP \(bu 2
The audit triggers are in \fBdb/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR; a site that does
not use the API or the signer does not apply them and logs nothing. Persistent
triggers can only read their own database, so the actor lives in the zone file:
\fBkaridns_audit_actor\fR has one row (principal, change set, database
role). SQLite3 has one writer at a time (\fBBEGIN IMMEDIATE\fR): the API writes
the actor row first in its transaction and deletes it last. A writer that does
not is logged with a NULL principal. \fBkaridns\-zone\fR writes its own row
(\fBkaridns\-zone:<OS user>\fR) and deletes it before the commit.
.IP \(bu 2
The auth file holds the principals, credentials, memberships, record policies
and grants. Its own changes are logged by triggers into \fBkaridns_auth_audit\fR
in the auth file. \fBThe auth file belongs to the user of the API and has mode
0600\fR; the loader's user must not be able to read it (it holds the password
hashes). Run \fBPRAGMA foreign_keys=ON\fR on the connection.
.IP \(bu 2
The checks that need no API are in the file: the \fBCHECK\fR constraints, users
only in \fBcredentials\fR, no cycle, depth 8, and the closure table. What
PostgreSQL does in policies and in the escalation trigger, the API must do on
SQLite3, and it must delete or rename the grants of a zone it deletes or
renames.
.SS The API tables, the zone generation and versions, and the check role (0.6.0 items D and E)
The API with permission checks, the zone templates and the zone versions add
these objects to schema version 3 (the version row stays 3: 0.6.0 is not
released). \fBThe server, \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fB and \fBkaricheck db\fB use
none of them\fR: they do not read the tables, and a database that has them
passes \fBkaricheck db\fR and the loader's schema check as it did without
them. The commands that use them are \fBkaridns\-zone apply\fR and \fBimport\fR
(generation, versions) and the WebAdmin API (\fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR: \fBGET
/auth/me\fR, the sessions, tokens, principals, policies, grants and the audit
log are these tables).
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_zone_state\fR
\fIWhere:\fR each data schema / the zone file
.br
\fIWhat it holds:\fR \fBdomain_id\fR, \fBgeneration\fR, \fBchanged_at\fR: the
\fBgeneration\fR of the zone. Written by the triggers of the owner only
(PostgreSQL: \fBkaridns_log_domains()\fR, the function that writes the change
log, grows it the first time a transaction changes a zone, so a row-by-row
import of a million records bumps it once; SQLite3: the trigger
\fBkaridns_changes_generation\fR on \fBkaridns_changes\fR, once per changed
row). It follows exactly the set of tables that write \fBkaridns_changes\fR. A
zone with no row has generation 0. \fBON DELETE CASCADE\fR.
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_zone_versions\fR, \fBkaridns_zone_version_rrsets\fR
\fIWhere:\fR each data schema / the zone file
.br
\fIWhat it holds:\fR The \fBversions\fR of a zone, one row per committed change
set: \fBversion\fR (per zone, from 1), \fBkind\fR (\fBcreate\fR, \fBedit\fR,
\fBtemplate\fR, \fBimport\fR, \fBrollback\fR), \fBchange_set\fR,
\fBprincipal_id\fR, \fBprincipal_name\fR, \fBdb_role\fR, \fBat\fR, \fBcomment\fR
(1 KiB), \fBserial_before\fR, \fBserial_after\fR, \fBgeneration_before\fR,
\fBgeneration_after\fR, \fBrollback_of\fR, \fBdelta_complete\fR; and the RRsets
the version changed (\fBowner\fR in the form of \fBrecords.name\fR, \fBtype\fR,
\fBbefore\fR and \fBafter\fR: canonical lines \fB<ttl> <TYPE> <rdata>\fR joined
by LF, NULL for absent). The newest 100 versions per zone are kept (a constant
in \fBkaridns_zone_versions_prune_fn\fR / the trigger
\fBkaridns_zone_versions_prune\fR). Not audited: they are derived from the
audited rows.
.TP 8
\fBsessions\fR, \fBapi_tokens\fR
\fIWhere:\fR \fBkaridns_auth\fR / the auth file
.br
\fIWhat it holds:\fR Browser sessions and API tokens: the SHA-256 of the cookie
(\fBid_hash\fR) and of the CSRF token (\fBcsrf_hash\fR), \fBprincipal_id\fR,
times (\fBcreated_at\fR, \fBlast_seen\fR, \fBexpires_at\fR), \fBtotp_ok\fR,
\fBclient\fR; for a token its \fBname\fR, \fBsecret_hash\fR, \fBread_only\fR,
\fBexpires_at\fR (at most 366 days after \fBcreated_at\fR), \fBlast_used_at\fR,
\fBrevoked_at\fR. Deleted with their principal. The audit writes the hashes as
\fB<redacted>\fR and does not log a change of \fBlast_seen\fR alone.
.TP 8
\fBoidc_states\fR
\fIWhere:\fR \fBkaridns_auth\fR / the auth file
.br
\fIWhat it holds:\fR The state of a sign-in with an OpenID Connect provider that
has started and not come back (item F, milestone M5): the SHA-256 of the
\fBstate\fR parameter (the key) and of the cookie that ties the flow to the
browser, the provider's name, the nonce, the PKCE code verifier, the route to
return to, \fBcreated_at\fR, \fBexpires_at\fR (ten minutes; at most an hour by a
constraint). Used once: read and deleted by the callback. Not
audited. \fBPostgreSQL: the API role has no privilege on the table\fR; it calls
the SECURITY DEFINER functions \fBoidc_state_put(...)\fR (false when 1000 are
waiting), \fBoidc_state_take(state_hash)\fR (returns the row of an unexpired
state and deletes it) and \fBoidc_lookup(issuer, subject)\fR (the id of the
user, not disabled, whom the identity \fBcredentials.oidc_issuer\fR,
\fBoidc_subject\fR is linked to), and \fBsweep_expired()\fR deletes the states
that are over.
.TP 8
\fBtemplates\fR, \fBtemplate_records\fR
\fIWhere:\fR \fBkaridns_auth\fR / the auth file
.br
\fIWhat it holds:\fR Zone data templates: name, description, \fBrevision\fR
(grows by one with every change of the template or of a record), the SOA
defaults (\fBsoa_mname\fR, \fBsoa_rname\fR, \fBsoa_refresh\fR, \fBsoa_retry\fR,
\fBsoa_expire\fR, \fBsoa_minimum\fR); the records \fB(template_id, ordinal)\fR
with \fBowner\fR (\fB@\fR or a relative name in lower case, no final dot),
\fBtype\fR (no DNSSEC type), \fBttl\fR, \fBcontent\fR. Written by a global
master, read by every principal; audited.
.PP
\fBRow level security (PostgreSQL).\fR \fBsessions\fR and \fBapi_tokens\fR: the
principal's own rows, a global master all of them. \fBtemplates\fR and
\fBtemplate_records\fR: everybody reads, a global master
writes. \fBkaridns_zone_state\fR: readable with a grant on the
zone. \fBkaridns_zone_versions\fR: readable with a grant on the zone, insertable
by the principal itself (\fBprincipal_id = karidns.principal\fR);
\fBkaridns_zone_version_rrsets\fR: readable and insertable by the rule of
\fBrr_visible\fR (a subdomain master reads the history of its names
only). Nobody with an API policy updates or deletes a version. A request that
carries only a cookie or a token cannot find its row under those policies, so
two SECURITY DEFINER lookups return the row of an unexpired credential of a
principal that is not disabled and nothing else:
\fBkaridns_auth.session_lookup(id_hash)\fR and
\fBkaridns_auth.api_token_lookup(id, secret_hash)\fR (\fBEXECUTE\fR is granted
to the API role). A third SECURITY DEFINER function,
\fBkaridns_auth.sweep_expired()\fR, deletes the sessions whose absolute time is
over, of every principal (the policy shows the API role the rows of its own
principal only): the hourly sweep of the API calls it. The API role also gets
\fBSELECT\fR on \fBkaridns_server_status\fR. A fourth SECURITY DEFINER function,
\fBkaridns_auth.prune_audit(days)\fR (0.6.1), deletes the audit rows older than
\fBdays\fR days (at least 7, the oldest 10,000 a call): no role has a right on
\fBaudit_log\fR, so this is how the WebAdmin's option \fBaudit\-retention\fR
prunes it; it is granted to the API role only.
.PP
\fBWhy the generation counts every change.\fR The signer's re-sign writes DNSSEC
records, the ordername and auth of other records, the SOA serial and its own
metadata, and the trigger counts them like any other change; skipping some rows
would need the content of an SOA to be parsed in SQL on both databases and would
leave a hole in "every writer bumps it". The price is that a signed zone can
make a commit run of the API fail with "changed" once per signing, which the API
repeats; the "changed outside the API" mark of the version list (a gap between
\fBgeneration_after\fR of one version and \fBgeneration_before\fR of the next)
is therefore noisy on signed zones and the API ignores a gap in which only
DNSSEC rows and the SOA serial changed.
.PP
\fBThe check role (\fBdb/karidns_check_role.pgsql.sql\fB).\fR A change set of a
subdomain master is checked against the whole zone, and row level security shows
the API's session only the part its principal has a grant on. The API therefore
runs \fBkaridns\-zone apply \-\-dry\-run\fR as a \fBcheck role\fR: a member of
\fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR (it sees every row and passes every write policy) that
\fBcan never commit\fR. The script makes the role a member of the group role
\fBkaridns_never_commit\fR and attaches to every table the dry run writes
(\fBdomains\fR, \fBrecords\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR, the \fBkaridns_*\fR zone
tables, \fBkaridns_changes\fR, \fBkaridns_zone_state\fR, the version tables,
\fBkaridns_key_requests\fR, and \fBkaridns_auth.audit_log\fR) a deferred
constraint trigger, \fBkaridns_auth.enable_never_commit(schema)\fR, whose
function raises at COMMIT for a session whose login role (\fBsession_user\fR) is
a member of \fBkaridns_never_commit\fR and is not a superuser. The test is two
built-in calls in the \fBWHEN\fR condition, so other roles pay almost
nothing. \fBSET ROLE\fR, \fBSET CONSTRAINTS ALL IMMEDIATE\fR and a \fBPREPARE
TRANSACTION\fR do not get around it (the last fires deferred triggers; keep
\fBmax_prepared_transactions\fR at 0 anyway), and the role cannot drop the
triggers (it owns nothing). The script refuses a superuser, the owner of the
tables (or a member of it) and a member of an API role
(\fBkaridns_auth.assert_check_role()\fR), grants the writer role's privileges
(by running \fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR for the role), \fBUSAGE\fR on
\fBkaridns_auth\fR and \fBEXECUTE\fR on \fBkaridns_auth.bump_serial\fR. Use a
login role of its own for it. \fBkaridns_auth.disable_never_commit(schema)\fR
removes the triggers; do it before you drop \fBkaridns_never_commit\fR.
.PP
\fBMigration.\fR There is no new schema version. A database made before these
objects (version 2 or version 3) gets them when the files are applied again:
PostgreSQL \fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR (once per data schema, as the owner;
it creates \fBkaridns_zone_state\fR with a row of generation 0 for every zone,
the version tables, the \fBkaridns_auth\fR tables and functions, the new
triggers and policies, and replaces \fBkaridns_log_domains()\fR), then the role
scripts again (\fBdb/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql\fR,
\fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR, \fBdb/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR,
the new \fBdb/karidns_check_role.pgsql.sql\fR; they grant on the new tables and
create the policies; the loader role needs nothing, it already reads every
\fBkaridns_*\fR table); SQLite3 \fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR on the zone
file, \fBdb/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR if the audit is used, and
\fBdb/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR on the auth file. Applied again, every file
only adds objects. \fBtests/run_db_schema_migrate_test.sh\fR runs it.
.SS What RLS and the audit cost
Measured on 2026-11-06 with \fBtests/perf/auth_rls_bench.sh\fR on the FreeBSD
15.1 build host (4 vCPU, shared with other jails), PostgreSQL 18.6 with
\fBshared_buffers\fR 128 MB, \fBfsync\fR off, a private cluster. Each figure is
the median of 3 runs, each run a new connection. The big zone has 100,000
records; the user is in a group that is domain master of it. Times are the
client's, with the rows sent to \fB/dev/null\fR.
.PP
