T}	T{
80 ms (13 ms with the audit off)
T}	\&166 ms (156 ms)	\&
.TE
.PP
The owner's change sets above have the audit triggers on (the API scripts switch
them on); the figures in brackets are the same measurement with the other
100,000 zones and 10,000 grants. The cost of RLS on a read is the checks that
make a row visible: the name must be inside the zone (and, for a subdomain
master, inside its suffix). Writes are checked row by row with the full
rules. The grants are read once per statement, not once per row; the lookups use
the indexes \fBgrants (data_schema, zone_name)\fR and \fBgrants
(principal_id)\fR and the key of \fBprincipal_closure\fR (an index-only scan of
0.02 ms, the whole lookup 0.04 ms). The measurement used 100,000 zones and
10,000 grants because the host is shared; the lookups are by index, so more
zones should not change them.
.PP
\fBkaridns\-zone import\fR (replace mode, \fB\-\-serial keep\fR) of the same
100,000 records as the writer role, a zone that already has them (so 100,000
rows are deleted and inserted again), RLS on:
.PP
.TS
lbw(40) lb lb
lw(40) l l.
\&Audit	\&Time	\&Audit rows
_
T{
off (\fBkaridns_auth.disable_audit('public')\fR)
T}	\&17.2 s	\&0
\&on, \fB\-\-audit rows\fR	\&36.1 s (2.1 times)	\&200,000
\&on, \fB\-\-audit summary\fR	\&35.7 s	\&2
.TE
.PP
The audit costs about 0.1 ms per changed row (the row trigger builds the JSON of
the old and the new row and looks up the zone) or, in summary mode, about 0.2 ms
per statement (the importer inserts one row per statement). Summary mode saves
the space of the log, not the time. Both figures include the statement triggers
with transition tables and the maintenance triggers that run as the owner (the
change log; the trigger of the signer is described next).
.PP
\fBThe trigger of the signer and what it costs the others.\fR The trigger
\fBkaridns_signer_soa_upd\fR on \fBrecords\fR is \fBBEFORE UPDATE OF <every
column but ordername and auth> FOR EACH ROW\fR, so an \fBUPDATE\fR of just
\fBordername\fR and \fBauth\fR (the signer's own, \fBpdnsutil
rectify\-zone\fR's) never calls it. The column list is part of the trigger (it
is read from the table when \fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR is applied, so a
column a later PowerDNS adds is covered when the file is applied again), and
PostgreSQL will not drop a listed column of \fBrecords\fR while the trigger
exists: \fBDROP TRIGGER karidns_signer_soa_upd ON records\fR first, then apply
the file again. For any other \fBUPDATE\fR it runs once per row for \fBevery\fR
role, the operator's and PowerDNS's too, and asks
\fBkaridns_auth.is_signer_caller()\fR first: is the login role or the \fBSET
ROLE\fR role one of the rows of \fBkaridns_auth.signer_roles\fR or a member of
one? That function does not run a recursive query:
\fBpg_has_role(.., 'MEMBER')\fR walks the memberships through the backend's own
cache, a superuser (the operator; the role scripts refuse a superuser as the
signer) is answered from the server's \fBis_superuser\fR setting (with \fBSET
ROLE\fR in use the login role's \fBrolsuper\fR is looked up, because
\fBpg_has_role\fR says yes for every superuser), and the answer is \fBnot\fR
cached in a transaction-local setting: any session can set a custom setting, so
a signer could write "I am no signer" before its first \fBUPDATE\fR. For a
signer the row is then compared as JSON without \fBordername\fR and \fBauth\fR,
and an SOA row is held to its serial. Measured on PostgreSQL 18, FreeBSD,
100,000 rows of \fBrecords\fR (one \fBUPDATE\fR of all of them by the owner,
median of five runs after a \fBVACUUM\fR): \fBSET ttl = ttl + 1\fR: 4.3 s with
the former trigger (a \fBWHEN\fR clause with \fBcanon_type()\fR and two
\fBto_jsonb()\fR per row, and a recursive query in \fBis_signer_caller()\fR),
0.90 s now, 0.69 s with no signer trigger at all; \fBSET content = content\fR:
2.1 s, 0.87 s, 0.69 s; \fBSET ordername = .., auth = ..\fR: 2.1 s, 0.72 s (the
trigger does not fire), 0.69 s.
.PP
\fBA re-sign writes a few change-log rows and \fBNOTIFY\fBs.\fR \fBkaridns\-zone
import \-\-signer\fR does not write a record per statement: the DNSSEC records
are deleted by a \fBDELETE ... WHERE id = ANY(...)\fR, inserted by one
\fBINSERT ... SELECT FROM unnest(...)\fR and the ordername and auth of the other
records updated by one \fBUPDATE ... FROM unnest(...)\fR (a statement for every
20,000 rows or 200,000 ids). The change log (\fBkaridns_changes\fR) and the
audit in summary mode are written by statement triggers, so a zone of 2000
hosts, signed again, gives 5 rows in \fBkaridns_changes\fR and 3 in the audit
log (\fBtests/run_karidns_zone_pg_test.sh\fR), and \fBNOTIFY karidns_changes\fR
a few times, not once per record. SQLite3 runs the single-row statements: its
triggers fire per row, so the change log has a row per changed row there (one
write transaction, one \fBNOTIFY\fR\-less poll of \fBPRAGMA data_version\fR for
the loader) and the audit a row per row.
.SH PACKAGES
\fBpackaging/freebsd/build_pkg.sh \-\-db none|sqlite3|postgresql|both\fR builds
four FreeBSD packages from the same server and tools; only the loader and its
dependencies differ. They install the same files, so they \fBconflicts\fR with
each other (install one).
.PP
.TS
lb lb lbw(33)
l l lw(33).
\&Package	\&\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR	\&Dependencies added
_
\&\fBkaridns\fR	\&not installed	\&none
\&\fBkaridns\-sqlite3\fR	\&SQLite3 driver	\&\fBdatabases/sqlite3\fR
\&\fBkaridns\-postgresql\fR	\&PostgreSQL driver	T{
\fBdatabases/postgresqlNN\-client\fR (libpq; NN is the version the build host
linked)
T}
\&\fBkaridns\-db\fR	\&both drivers	\&both
.TE
.PP
The database packages install \fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-dbloader\fR
(root:wheel, mode 0555), \fBkaridns\-zone\fR
(\fB/usr/local/bin/karidns\-zone\fR, with the same drivers, and its manual),
this manual, and the SQL files of their drivers in
\fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/\fR. The signer daemon \fBkaridns\-signer\fR is a
package of its own (\fBkaridns\-signer\fR, Distribution Guide, 2.6). In a
package without the loader \fBkaricheck db\fR reports \fBDB support is not
installed\fR. See Distribution Guide.
