.PP
Notes on PostgreSQL:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe start\fR is 30% (\fBz10k\fR) and 15% (\fBz1m\fR) slower than with SQLite3
and the first answer comes 1-1.3 s later; the loader reading alone takes 29.0 s
/ 34.4 s (see the thread sweep), so the Backend adds the same 2-3.5 s as
before. The goal (answering within 60 s) is met.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA reload that compares everything is slower than with SQLite3.\fR Where the
time goes (a timestamp at every step of the loader, \fBz1m\fR, nothing changed,
4.2 s): 1.95 s are in the Backend before the loader is asked anything
(\fBkarictl reconfig\fR alone takes the same 1.95 s: the new snapshot is built
from the 1,000,000 database zones of the registry, a cost of the Backend that
has nothing to do with the database driver and that SQLite3 pays too), 2.0 s in
the zone list (the query is 1.7 s in \fBpsql\fR: one pass over the 11,000,000
records for the 1,000,000 SOA rows), 0.25 s in the comparison with the Backend's
inventory, 0.03 s to free the list. At \fBz10k\fR the same steps take 0.2 s in
all.
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe zone list.\fR It used to join each domain to its apex SOA on
\fBlower(r.name) = lower(d.name)\fR, which PostgreSQL could answer only by
reading the records of every zone (0.8 s at \fBz10k\fR, 2.0 s at \fBz1m\fR), and
the list is read by every full comparison, so by every auto batch. It joins on
the plain name now (\fBr.name = d.name\fR, served by the standard indexes on
\fBrecords\fR), with the case-insensitive comparison as a per-zone fallback for
a zone whose SOA owner differs in case (which the schema's
\fBc_lowercase_name\fR constraint forbids), and the loader plans the list for
all of its rows (\fBSET LOCAL cursor_tuple_fraction = 1\fR for this one query; a
cursor is planned for its first 10% by default): 0.2 s at \fBz10k\fR (the reload
without changes 0.8 s to 0.22 s, the auto reload 0.95 s to 0.42 s, of which 0.2
s is \fBcoalesce\fR) and 1.8 s at \fBz1m\fR (the planner reads the SOA rows once
there; 2.0 s before). If one zone has SOA records of two spellings of its name,
the list takes the lowest \fBrecords.id\fR of the exact spelling (before: of all
spellings); that is a data error anyway, as for \fBdomains.name\fR below. The
SQLite3 driver now orders the same way (\fBORDER BY d.id, r.id\fR: the smallest
\fBrecords.id\fR among several enabled SOAs, explicitly).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe changed zones.\fR A reload that changes fewer zones than
\fBbulk\-threshold\fR percent (default 25) of the source reads them without
scanning everything. A zone read on its own costs a statement per table and
three round trips per statement (\fBDECLARE\fR, \fBFETCH\fR, \fBCLOSE\fR): about
fifteen with the extension tables, 0.55 ms per zone on the local socket
(\fBz1m\fR, 100,000 changed zones: metadata 7.1 s, records 11.4 s, tags and
tinydns attributes 29.6 s, conversion 6.5 s of the 54.6 s the loader spent). The
PostgreSQL driver now has \fBscan_records_in\fR, \fBscan_metadata_in\fR and
\fBscan_ext_in\fR: up to 128 changed zones (consecutive in the list) are read
with one statement per table (\fBdomain_id = ANY ($1::bigint[])\fR), the records
still streamed through the cursor, so the loader holds the metadata and
extension rows of 128 zones and the records of one, as before. The zones are
converted and sent in the same order, the stream is the same bytes
(\fBtests/test_db_pgsql.c\fR compares the group calls with the single-zone calls
row by row; \fBtests/run_db_pg_group_reload_test.sh\fR runs 160 changed zones
among 700). The loader spent 11.0 s instead of 54.6 s on the 100,000 zones; the
whole reload 14-15 s instead of 60 s. \fBbulk\-threshold\fR keeps its meaning
(the share of zones above which one scan of all records is cheaper than reading
the changed ones); only the cost of reading below it fell, so the break-even
moved: for PostgreSQL a higher threshold than 25% may now pay. A reload of one
zone (\fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR, \fB\-\-zone\fR) is read as before. SQLite3
reads zone by zone (a statement costs no round trip there). With
\fBloader\-threads\fR above 1 each worker reads groups of up to 64 zones.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhat is left.\fR The reload without changes at \fBz1m\fR (4.2 s) is the
Backend's 1.95 s, the list's 2.0 s and the comparison's 0.25 s; the list could
only be made much cheaper by not comparing the SOA serials, which the design
keeps as the safety net behind the change log, and the PostgreSQL server of the
host has \fBmax_parallel_workers_per_gather 0\fR. A reload that changes 10% of
10,000 zones of 1,000 records (4.3-4.6 s) is dominated by converting and sending
1,000,000 records, not by round trips. More \fBloader\-threads\fR (each thread
opens its own connection, mind \fBmax_connections\fR) help both, as in the
thread sweep.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBfind_zone\fB for a zone that is not there\fR (a deletion, or a typo in
\fBkarictl reload\fR): the exact-spelling query uses the index on
\fBdomains.name\fR (0.04-0.2 ms at 1,000,000 zones, whether the zone exists or
not); when it finds nothing, the second query with \fBlower(d.name)\fR scans the
table: 189-218 ms at 1,000,000 zones (5 runs with \fBpsql\fR; \fBSeq Scan on
domains\fR), which is most of the 272 ms of the delete above. It is the price of
finding a zone stored with capital letters; PowerDNS stores names in lower case,
and \fBkaricheck db\fR warns about two rows that differ only in case. An index
\fBCREATE INDEX ON domains (lower(name))\fR would make the fallback an index
search too; the loader does not need it and does not create it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAuto reload\fR with \fBLISTEN\fR sees a commit at once; the figures above are
the comparison that follows. A hot standby refuses \fBLISTEN\fR; see below.
.PP
\fBA PostgreSQL hot standby (streaming replica).\fR Tried with a scratch primary
and a standby of it on the build host (two throwaway clusters under their own
directory, ports 5442 and 5443, started by the \fBpostgres\fR user, removed
afterwards): 50 zones, \fBkaridns\fR reading the standby over the UNIX socket as
an ordinary role. \fBLISTEN\fR is refused (\fBcannot execute LISTEN during
recovery\fR), and the loader behaves as follows.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIReload requests\fR (\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR, written on the primary and
replicated): the loader logs once that it cannot \fBLISTEN karidns_reload\fR and
reads the requests every \fBpoll\-interval\fR; a request inserted on the primary
is applied after 0.6 s (replication lag, the timer, one zone read). \fBkarictl
reload\fR works as on a primary.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIThe status row\fR (\fBkaridns_server_status\fR) cannot be written on a
read-only server (\fBcannot set transaction read\-write mode during
recovery\fR): the loader logs it once (it stayed one line for four requests),
\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows it as the last error of the reload requests
(\fBstatus written never\fR), the requests are still applied and the server
keeps answering.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIAuto reload\fR (\fBauto\-reload yes\fR) did not work on a standby before: the
loader's change detector needed the notification, so every poll failed
(\fBcannot watch the database: ... LISTEN\fR, \fBlast poll never\fR in
\fBdbstatus\fR) and no change was ever picked up. The PostgreSQL driver now
reads a fingerprint of \fBkaridns_changes\fR (the number of rows and the highest
\fBseq\fR) every \fBpoll\-interval\fR when \fBLISTEN\fR is refused, the loader
logs \fBthe server refuses LISTEN (a standby?); the change log is read every N s
instead\fR once, and a zone added on the primary is served after 0.75 s, a
changed record after 1.0 s (\fBpoll\-interval 1\fR, \fBcoalesce 200\fR, local
replication). The cost is one index-only \fBcount(*)\fR per poll over at most
1,000,000 log rows. \fBtests/test_db_pgsql.c\fR tests the fingerprint (a commit,
a row with a lower \fBseq\fR that commits last, a lost connection) by setting
the driver's \fBlisten_failed\fR flag; the standby itself is a manual procedure:
\fBpg_basebackup \-R\fR of a primary with \fBwal_level replica\fR, the standby
started with its own \fB\-p\fR, the database block pointing at the standby's
socket and port.
.PP
