\fIMet:\fR yes
.TP 8
T-RECOVER, loader killed: answers continue; new loader connected / has compared every zone
\fIz10k:\fR 1.06 s / 24.4 s
.br
\fIz1m:\fR 1.1 s / 29.4 s
.TP 8
\fBkarictl reload\fR, nothing changed
\fIz10k:\fR 0.04 s
.br
\fIz1m:\fR 3.3 s
.TP 8
\fBkarictl reload\fR, 1% of the zones edited
\fIz10k:\fR 0.29 s
.br
\fIz1m:\fR 3.9 s
.TP 8
\fBkarictl reload\fR, 10% of the zones edited, under load (see below)
\fIz10k:\fR 2.6 s
.br
\fIz1m:\fR 17.7 s
.PP
Notes:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThere are no persisted images in this release\fR, so "restart" is a start:
the whole database is read again. T-START is met without them for 10^7 records
and for 10^6 zones on this host; the time is the time the loader needs to read
and convert the database (\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-dump\fR alone: 23.6 s for
\fBz10k\fR, 27.9 s for \fBz1m\fR with one thread), the Backend adds about 0.5 s
(\fBz10k\fR) and 5 s (\fBz1m\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA restarted loader reads the whole database\fR (it compares every zone with
the Backend's inventory before its first batch; 24 s / 29 s above). The Backend
answers from its zones all the time. T-RECOVER for a killed Backend was measured
by starting the server again immediately after it exited (it exits 0.06-0.16 s
after the kill).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBT-ZONEADD at 1,000,000 zones.\fR The first measurement missed the target
because a one-zone reload read the list of all zones of the source. The loader
now looks the zone up by name (\fBfind_zone\fR of the drivers, an index on
\fBdomains.name\fR; PostgreSQL falls back to \fBlower()\fR only when the exact
spelling is not found) and asks the inventory for that one name. What remained
(an add 0.17 s, a delete 0.2-0.6 s at 1,000,000 zones) was profiled in the beta
leftovers: 58 ms were still in the loader, because the SQLite3 \fBfind_zone\fR
was written \fBname = ? OR name = ?\fR with an explicit \fBCOLLATE\fR, for which
SQLite chooses \fBSCAN domains\fR (8,400 pages) instead of the index; it is an
\fBIN\fR list now (\fBEXPLAIN QUERY PLAN\fR: \fBSEARCH name_index\fR). About 105
ms were in the Backend: \fBsnapshot_apply_db_delta()\fR copied the zone list of
the view and rebuilt both hash tables from every name (90 ms of the 105; the
delta of the first second of a start does the same with 25,000 zones). It now
copies the hash tables of the old snapshot (\fBmemcpy\fR) and patches them: an
added zone is appended and linked, a removed one is unlinked and the last entry
takes its slot, so the work per change is a chain walk; the tables are rebuilt
only when the load factor would pass one half, or when a catalog consumer
leaves. What was still proportional to the number of zones was the copy of the
tables (about 30 MB at 1,000,000 zones) and one reference count per zone for the
new snapshot (and one release per zone when the old snapshot was freed by its
cleanup thread): \fBsnapshot_apply_db_delta()\fR took 19 ms instead of 105
ms. D1a (the zone directory) removed that: the views of a snapshot share one
directory per view, \fBsnapshot_apply_db_delta()\fR inserts and removes the
changed elements in place (open addressing; a tombstone for a removal, a bigger
table only when the load would pass one half) and publishes a snapshot that only
holds the names, the match-clients and a reference of each directory, so the
work is proportional to the change. What leaves the directory is freed when the
snapshot that was current at that time and every older one are drained. A new
entry that replaces a served one (a database zone that changes kind, for example
from primary to secondary) takes the place of the old one in the directory at
the commit, not at the next delta. The other 10-20 ms of an add are the loader's
read, the stream and the first query. Of about 40 single reloads one in twenty
was delayed by about 0.5 s (0.56 s once, 0.57 s before the change); it also
happened for a change, which publishes no delta, and the cause was not found.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe 0.13 / 0.26 / 0.52 s one-zone reloads\fR (about one in twenty at
1,000,000 zones, with either source, add, change or delete, delta or not) had
two causes in the Backend, both fixed after B13: the control thread's one-second
tick walked every zone of the snapshot inside an RCU read section (90-250 ms at
1,000,000 zones) looking for secondaries to refresh and for NOTIFY requests, so
a \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR that arrived during the walk waited for it, and
the grace period of the batch (\fBzone_retire_process\fR) waited for that read
section with sleeps that doubled up to 131 ms, which made a walk of 70 ms cost
131 ms and one of 250 ms cost 524 ms. The tick now looks only at the view's list
of secondaries and catalog members (\fBtick_entries\fR, built with the hash
tables and inherited by the one-zone delta), walks every zone only when a NOTIFY
was requested since its last full walk, and the grace-period wait sleeps at most
1 ms at a time. Measured afterwards with 100 one-zone changes at 1,000,000 zones
(SQLite3): \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR answered in 5-11 ms and the change was
served 15-25 ms after the command (the tail up to 77 ms is the \fBdag\fR process
of the measurement; 0 ms of query time). The tick also no longer costs 10-25% of
a core at 1,000,000 zones.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBT-AUTORELOAD\fR depends on \fBpoll\-interval\fR (SQLite3) and \fBcoalesce\fR;
the table uses 1 s and 200 ms. With PostgreSQL a commit is seen by \fBLISTEN\fR
at once; the latency is then the \fBcoalesce\fR window plus the comparison, see
the PostgreSQL table below.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReload under load.\fR \fBdnsperf \-c 4 \-T 2 \-q 200\fR ran for 20 s over
random names of the data set while 10% of the zones were edited and \fBkarictl
reload\fR was started 4 s in. Queries per second before the reload / mean /
lowest second during it: \fBz10k\fR 550k / 198k / 6.6k, \fBz1m\fR 403k / 227k /
12k; no query was lost and the longest latency was 0.56 s (\fBz10k\fR) and 1.2 s
(\fBz1m\fR). Queries are still answered, but throughput falls while the reload
is busy, in some seconds nearly to zero: on 4 vCPU the load generator, the two
query threads, the Backend's builder and the loader compete for the same cores,
so part of this is the test setup; the T-RELOAD target (p99 within a small
factor of idle) was not measured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMemory.\fR About 108 bytes per record (\fBz10k\fR) and 3.0 KB per zone of 11
records (\fBz1m\fR) in the Backend; the loader stays at 15 MB for a few large
zones and needs about 300 MB for the inventory and the zone list of 1,000,000
zones. A reload that edits 10% of the zones keeps the old versions of the
changed zones until they are freed: the Backend peaked 82 MB above its idle size
at \fBz10k\fR (1,000 zones of 1,000 records) and 34 MB at \fBz1m\fR. Each extra
\fBloader\-thread\fR adds about 25-40 MB to the loader with large zones (15, 53,
93 MB for 1, 2, 4 threads at \fBz10k\fR) and little with small ones.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBT-QPS (L)\fR (no database block, \fBtests/karidns\-test.conf\fR, \fBdnsperf
\-c 4 \-T 2 \-q 200 \-l 20\fR, three runs, host idle): the v0.6 beta branch 475k
(median of 6 runs; 495k and 461k in the two rounds) against 483k for \fBmain\fR
before the database work (484k and 465k), the figure of 2026-10-09 being
481k. The spread between runs of one build (445k-508k) is larger than the
difference: no regression.
.PP
\fBThread sweep.\fR Start of \fBkaridns\fR with each combination, in seconds
until \fBinitial load complete\fR (one run each; reloads in the full results):
.PP
.TS
rb rb rb rb rbw(11) rbw(12)
r r r r rw(11) rw(12).
\&\fBingest\-threads\fR	\&\fBloader\-threads\fR	\&\fBz10k\fR	\&\fBz1m\fR	T{
\fBz10k\fR reload, 10% changed, under load
T}	T{
\fBz1m\fR reload, 10% changed, under load
T}
_
\&1	\&1	\&24.1	\&33.8	\&2.6 s	\&14.3 s
\&1	\&2	\&14.7	\&21.0	\&1.5 s	\&8.7 s
\&1	\&4	\&15.3	\&21.2	\&1.6 s	\&9.0 s
\&2	\&1	\&24.1	\&34.8	\&2.7 s	\&16.9 s
\&2	\&2	\&12.8	\&19.2	\&1.4 s	\&10.1 s
\&2	\&4	\&10.9	\&19.0	\&1.3 s	\&7.6 s
\&4	\&1	\&24.1	\&34.4	\&2.9 s	\&15.0 s
\&4	\&2	\&14.2	\&20.0	\&1.4 s	\&10.7 s
\&4	\&4	\&10.5	\&20.1	\&1.2 s	\&11.9 s
.TE
.PP
The loader alone (\fB\-\-dump\fR, 1 / 2 / 4 threads): \fBz10k\fR 23.6 / 12.4 /
6.7 s and \fBz1m\fR 27.9 / 15.2 / 8.3 s with SQLite3; 29.0 / 15.5 / 8.6 s and
34.4 / 19.6 / 11.5 s with PostgreSQL 18.6 on the same host (database of 2.6 GB
for \fBz1m\fR). The start of \fBkaridns\fR with a PostgreSQL source is in the
next section.
.PP
\fBRecommended values\fR (the defaults stay 1, so a small host or a database of
a few zones needs no tuning):
.IP \(bu 2
The loader is the bottleneck of a start and of a big reload: with
\fBloader\-threads 1\fR, more \fBingest\-threads\fR change nothing (24 s, 34 s
above). \fB\fBloader\-threads 2\fB\fR takes about 40% off (\fBz10k\fR 24.1 to
14.7 s, \fBz1m\fR 33.8 to 21.0 s).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBingest\-threads 2\fB together with \fBloader\-threads 2\fB\fR is the best
general setting on 4 cores: 12.8 s and 19.2 s, nearly the best of the sweep, and
it leaves two cores for the query threads. \fBloader\-threads 4\fR gains up to
another 15% with large zones (\fBz10k\fR 10.9 s) and nothing with many small
ones (\fBz1m\fR 19.0 s), and takes the cores the query threads need while a
reload runs. \fBingest\-threads 4\fR gave nothing over 2.
.IP \(bu 2
Rule of thumb: \fBloader\-threads\fR and \fBingest\-threads\fR of about half the
number of cores, never more than the cores minus the query threads; 1 on hosts
with one or two cores. \fBingest\-threads\fR is read when the server starts.
.IP \(bu 2
For PostgreSQL the same holds; each loader thread opens its own connection, so
check \fBmax_connections\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
The examples were run once with the package \fBkaridns\-db\fR installed under a
scratch root, so \fB/usr/local/...\fR below is a path of that installation
(\fBsqlite3\fR is the SQLite3 command line tool, \fBschema.sqlite3.sql\fR the
PowerDNS schema file; names are test names). \fBexample.test\fR and
\fB192.0.2.0/24\fR are reserved for documentation.
.PP
\fBA SQLite3 database with one zone.\fR
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ sqlite3 zones.sqlite3 < schema.sqlite3.sql
wal
$ sqlite3 zones.sqlite3 < /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql
$ cat zone.sql
INSERT INTO domains (name, type) VALUES ('example.test', 'NATIVE');
INSERT INTO records (domain_id, name, type, content, ttl, prio, disabled)
  SELECT id, 'example.test', 'SOA', 'ns1.example.test. hostmaster.example.test. 1 3600 600 86400 300', 3600, 0, 0 FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';
INSERT INTO records (domain_id, name, type, content, ttl, prio, disabled)
  SELECT id, 'example.test', 'NS', 'ns1.example.test.', 3600, 0, 0 FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';
INSERT INTO records (domain_id, name, type, content, ttl, prio, disabled)
  SELECT id, 'www.example.test', 'A', '192.0.2.10', 300, 0, 0 FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';
INSERT INTO records (domain_id, name, type, content, ttl, prio, disabled)
  SELECT id, 'ns1.example.test', 'A', '192.0.2.1', 3600, 0, 0 FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';
$ sqlite3 zones.sqlite3 < zone.sql
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBWhich drivers, which version.\fR
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-drivers
sqlite3
postgresql
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-version
karidns\-dbloader 0.6.2
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBWrite the stream the Backend would get.\fR The bytes do not depend on the
number of loader threads, and \fB\-\-zone\fR reads one zone with a query of its
own:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-dump zones.kdb \-\-driver sqlite3 \-\-database\-file zones.sqlite3
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-dump zones4.kdb \-\-driver sqlite3 \-\-database\-file zones.sqlite3 \-\-loader\-threads 4 && cmp zones.kdb zones4.kdb
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-dump \- \-\-driver sqlite3 \-\-database\-file zones.sqlite3 \-\-zone example.test | wc \-c
     579
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBCheck the database before it goes live.\fR \fB\-\-check\fR prints lines of
the check protocol (\fBI\fR information, \fBZ\fR a zone, \fBE\fR an error,
\fBW\fR a warning, \fBR\fR the result: zones, errors, warnings), \fBkaricheck
db\fR prints the same in the style of \fBkaricheck zone\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-check \-\-driver sqlite3 \-\-database\-file zones.sqlite3
I               0       karidns_* schema version 2
Z       example.test.   1       primary *
R       1       0       0
$ cat karidns.conf
options {
    pid\-file "none";
    database { driver sqlite3; database\-file "/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3"; loader\-path "/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-dbloader"; acl\-ceiling { 192.0.2.0/24; }; };
};
$ karicheck db karidns.conf
[INFO] Loading config karidns.conf...
[INFO] Checking Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3' (the default view) with the rights of the user claude (uid 1002)
[INFO] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3': karidns_* schema version 2
[RESULT] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3': 1 zone(s) read, 0 error(s), 0 warning(s)
[OK] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3' is valid.
[INFO] Checked 1 database (1 zone). Errors: 0, warnings: 0
.fi
.RE
.PP
A row the loader would refuse (the exit status is 1; \fB\-\-dump\fR would send
the zone as \fBZONE_FAILED\fR):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ sqlite3 zones.sqlite3 "INSERT INTO records (domain_id, name, type, content, ttl, prio, disabled) SELECT id, 'bad.example.test', 'A', '192.0.2.999', 300, 0, 0 FROM domains WHERE name = 'example.test';"
$ karicheck db karidns.conf
[INFO] Loading config karidns.conf...
[INFO] Checking Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3' (the default view) with the rights of the user claude (uid 1002)
[INFO] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3': karidns_* schema version 2
[ERROR] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3': zone 'example.test.' (domains.id 1): records.id 5: invalid IPv4 address literal in A record
[RESULT] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3': 1 zone(s) read, 1 error(s), 0 warning(s)
[INFO] Checked 1 database (1 zone). Errors: 1, warnings: 0
[FAIL] Database file '/home/claude/b13doc/zones.sqlite3' has errors.
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPostgreSQL.\fR The role \fBkaridns_loader\fR was created by \fBCREATE ROLE
karidns_loader LOGIN;\fR, the PowerDNS tables exist in the database
\fBkaridns\fR, the extension file is applied by the owner of the tables, and the
role script by a superuser such as \fBpostgres\fR (it alters the role, which
needs more than the right to the tables). The loader then reads with the
privileges of that role only:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
$ psql \-d karidns \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql
$ psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_loader \-v schema=public \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql karidns
$ psql \-d karidns \-c "SET ROLE karidns_loader; SELECT content FROM cryptokeys"
SET
ERROR:  permission denied for table cryptokeys
$ cat conninfo
host=/tmp dbname=karidns
$ karidns\-dbloader \-\-check \-\-driver postgresql \-\-conninfo\-file conninfo
W               0       the role claude can read cryptokeys.content (the private keys); the loader needs only domain_id (db/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql grants just that)
I               0       karidns_* schema version 2
R       0       0       1
.fi
.RE
.PP
(The last \fB\-\-check\fR ran as the owner of the tables, hence the warning
about \fBcryptokeys.content\fR; with \fBuser=karidns_loader\fR in the conninfo
file and the loader running as that role it does not appear. The conninfo file
must have mode 0600 and belong to the user who runs the loader.)
